#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124316 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "0cb849bd-8a9f-5305-b086-62da1a10e54f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8226" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\dfrac{x^2}{9} + (y+1)^2 = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9} + (y+1)^2 = 1, \\qquad z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$ can vary arbitrarily.\n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2: \\dfrac{x^2}{9} + (y+1)^2 = 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2: \\dfrac{x^2}{3^2} + \\dfrac{(y+1)^2}{1^2} = 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(0,-1)$ with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124317 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/id #uuid "64fc4653-a2ba-5013-8c70-1f4f14e5f8be" :question/math-academy-id "e-8227" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\dfrac {x^2} 2 + \\dfrac {(y-2)^2}4 = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{0^{2}}{2} + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\ny - 2 &= ± 2 \\\\\ny &= 2 ± 2 \\\\\ny &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $y=0$ and $y=4$ in the $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/